{"id":"after-it-passed","title":"After it passed: what the client then received","question":"For each lobbied measure that passed, what did the client who sought it receive afterwards: an award in the measure itself, permits at the site, new contracts or City payments?","finding":"51 passed measures that a client lobbied for are followed by something on record for the client (54 client rows). In 6 the measure itself names the client as counterparty, and the titles of those measures name $248.7 million in all; that sum adds different kinds of amount (contract ceilings, settlements and other terms as each title gives them), not money paid. At 10 rezoned or specially zoned sites, building permits with a stated cost of $117.4 million were issued in the three years after. Of 34 clients with City payments on both sides of the passage date, 27 were paid more in the three years after than in the three before.","how":"Measures are the passed Board files tied to lobbying by file number, plus enacted ordinances tied by subject words (marked as inferred). Only clients whose contacts sought support, approval or amendment are followed. Award: the amount in the Board’s money file where the client is the named counterparty. Permits: building permits issued within three years of passage at the street address in the title of a zoning, special-district or development-agreement measure (appeals are left out), with the cost stated on the permit. Contracts: agreements in the City’s contract table with the client as prime contractor starting within three years of passage; the agreed value is the whole contract’s, summed over every member of its team. Payments: the client’s City payments in up to three full fiscal years before and after the fiscal year of passage (ledger years 2019 to 2026).","limits":"Only the award and the permits can be read as following from the measure, and even those need the file to confirm: an award was usually negotiated by a department before the Board voted, and a permit at an address may belong to another owner or an earlier approval. Contracts and payments after passage are listed because they are on record, not because the measure caused them; most belong to unrelated work, and City spending on outside payees rose throughout these years, so most payees were paid more later whatever they lobbied for. An amount naming the client can also be money the client pays the City, a settlement or a rent reduction. Fee waivers and tax relief granted by an ordinance leave no payment to follow. Lobbying is registered, and so is giving by a lobbyist’s clients and colleagues. An official can sponsor or vote for something a donor wants because the official already favoured it; a donor can give to an official because of what the official already stands for. A private payment for an official act cannot be seen in any of these records, and nothing here says one was made. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"file","t":"Board file","type":"link"},{"k":"title","t":"Short title","type":"text"},{"k":"passed","t":"Passed","type":"date"},{"k":"match","t":"Tied to lobbying by","type":"text"},{"k":"client","t":"Client","type":"org"},{"k":"firms","t":"Lobbyist","type":"text"},{"k":"sponsors","t":"Sponsors","type":"text"},{"k":"award","t":"Amount in the measure naming the client","type":"usd"},{"k":"permits","t":"Building permits at the site within three years","type":"int"},{"k":"permitCost","t":"Stated cost of those permits","type":"usd"},{"k":"contracts","t":"New City contracts within three years","type":"int"},{"k":"agreed","t":"Their agreed value","type":"usd"},{"k":"before","t":"City paid the client, three years before","type":"usd"},{"k":"after","t":"City paid the client, three years after","type":"usd"},{"k":"gaveSponsors","t":"Circle gave the sponsors","type":"usd"}],"rows":[{"